WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.820
Digital signatures help verify authenticity and integrity.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.280
A signer uses a private key to sign data.

3
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:15.820
A verifier uses the corresponding public key to check that the signed data has not changed and that it was signed by the holder of the private key.

4
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.920
Signing is not the same as encryption.

5
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.340
Signed data may still be readable.

6
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.160
Encryption is about confidentiality.

7
00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.700
Signing is about trust in origin and change detection.

8
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:35.060
Signatures and MACs are common in APIs, webhooks, software updates, artifacts, and message workflows.

9
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.720
The receiver should verify before trusting.

10
00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:46.300
That means checking the signature or MAC, the key or issuer, the intended use, and any freshness or replay protections required by the design.

11
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.600
A valid signature proves only what the signature scheme is designed to prove.

12
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:57.380
It does not automatically prove authorization, business validity, or user intent.

13
00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:08.540
Signed tokens at a high level often carry claims such as subject, issuer, audience, expiration, scopes, roles, or other context.

14
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:18.560
Token validation should check signature validity, issuer, audience, expiration, intended use, and any application-specific requirements as appropriate.

15
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:29.140
Accepting an unsigned token, trusting claims without validation, or ignoring expiration can affect many endpoints at once because tokens often sit at the front of API access.

16
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:32.300
Key rotation for signing keys requires planning.

17
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:40.400
Verifiers may need to trust old and new keys during a transition, tokens may remain valid for a period, and services must refresh key material safely.

18
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:47.220
Replay and freshness concepts also matter when signed messages can be captured and sent again.

19
00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:54.680
Signed data still requires authorization, input validation, and business-rule checks before the application acts on it.
